Task: produce ONE markdown mitigation playbook document, and nothing else (no
preamble, no commentary, no code fences around the whole thing). The document
MUST match the exact structure below. A deterministic Python validator will
reject your output if any of these constraints are violated:

- Every `##` section listed below must be present, in order, with the exact
  heading text.
- The `<!-- meta -->` block must contain the keys `slug`, `class_name`,
  `seen`, `created_from`, `last_updated`, each on its own line as `key: value`.
- Applicability, Mitigation Procedure, Post-Mitigation Verification, and
  Required Evidence must each be non-empty.
- Required Evidence must contain at least one `- [ ]` checkbox item, and
  every item there must be a `- [ ]` checkbox.
- The following vague phrases are FORBIDDEN anywhere in the document:
  "appropriate values", "as needed", "relevant configuration", "if applicable",
  "as appropriate". Use parameterized placeholders for incident-specific
  tokens — those are precise, not vague — and write concrete commands and
  shapes around them.

Authoring rules specific to a v1-from-success mitigation playbook:
- Mitigation Procedure steps must cite the SHAPE of patch commands the
  successful run actually used (e.g. `kubectl set env deploy/<DEPLOYMENT> -n
  <NAMESPACE> <ENV_VAR>=<VALUE>`, `kubectl patch svc <SERVICE> -n <NAMESPACE>
  --type=json -p '[{"op": "replace", "path": "/spec/ports/0/targetPort",
  "value": <PORT>}]'`). Use placeholders wherever the successful run had a
  specific name, namespace, or value.
- Post-Mitigation Verification steps must describe how to confirm the
  cluster reached the healthy steady state (e.g. all target pods Running,
  no restarts, expected log line printed). Parameterize over the same
  placeholders.
- Required Evidence items must be concrete CHECKS the runtime subagent will
  perform AFTER applying the fix, parameterized over the incident-specific
  tokens (e.g. "All target Deployment pods are Running with restartCount=0
  for at least 30 seconds"). NOT diagnosis-style "evidence the misconfig
  exists" — these are post-fix verification gates.
- Known Pitfalls and Failure Patterns may legitimately be empty in v1. If
  you have nothing real to put there, emit the section header followed by a
  single literal placeholder bullet `- (none observed yet)`. Do NOT invent
  fake pitfalls.
- The meta block MUST be filled exactly as:
    slug: {{ slug }}
    class_name: {{ class_name }}
    seen: 1
    created_from: success
    last_updated: {{ current_iso }}

